<p>
I have a small ritual when I need to find something in a file: jump to the top with <code>M-&lt;</code>, start <code>C-s</code>, and type the search string. It works, but it is a little fiddly. The top of the buffer is not where I am, and most of the time I do not actually need to start there. I just want the first match in the file, or I want to search forward from wherever point happens to be.
</p>


<div id="org84b3f26" class="figure">
<p><img loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded" src="/static/emacs/20261025153614-emacs--Stop-Going-to-the-Top-isearch-allow-motion.jpg" alt="20261025153614-emacs--Stop-Going-to-the-Top-isearch-allow-motion.jpg" width="100%" />
</p>
</div>

<p>
The built-in <code>isearch-allow-motion</code> option is kinda useful. It lets the ordinary motion keys <code>M-&lt;</code> and <code>M-&gt;</code> work as match navigation while an isearch is active. Type <code>C-s</code>, type the search string, and then press <code>M-&lt;</code> to jump to the first occurrence in the buffer or <code>M-&gt;</code> to jump to the last. The search stays active, so <code>C-s</code> and <code>C-r</code> keep working from the new position. No trip to the top, no restarting the search.
</p>

<p>
With <code>isearch-allow-motion</code> set to non-nil, isearch intercepts four motion commands and gives them a search-specific meaning:
</p>

<table class="table table-striped" border="2" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="6" rules="groups" frame="hsides">


<colgroup>
<col  class="org-left" />

<col  class="org-left" />

<col  class="org-left" />

<col  class="org-left" />
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" class="org-left">Key</th>
<th scope="col" class="org-left">Command</th>
<th scope="col" class="org-left">Default during isearch</th>
<th scope="col" class="org-left">With isearch-allow-motion</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class="org-left"><code>M-&lt;</code></td>
<td class="org-left"><code>beginning-of-buffer</code></td>
<td class="org-left">exits isearch, goes to buffer start</td>
<td class="org-left">jumps to first match in buffer</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left"><code>M-&gt;</code></td>
<td class="org-left"><code>end-of-buffer</code></td>
<td class="org-left">exits isearch, goes to buffer end</td>
<td class="org-left">jumps to last match in buffer</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left"><code>C-v</code></td>
<td class="org-left"><code>scroll-up-command</code></td>
<td class="org-left">exits isearch, scrolls forward</td>
<td class="org-left">jumps to first match after window</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td class="org-left"><code>M-v</code></td>
<td class="org-left"><code>scroll-down-command</code></td>
<td class="org-left">exits isearch, scrolls backward</td>
<td class="org-left">jumps to last match before window</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>
The search string is not changed, and you are still in isearch after the jump. That is the whole trick: the motion command has been repurposed from "move the buffer" to "move through the matches", but only while you are searching.
</p>

<p>
The option has been in Emacs since 28.1, so I would say generally there is probably a high chance you already have it.
</p>

<p>
Add one line in your init file:
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-elisp"><code>(<span style="color: #fb4933;">setq</span> isearch-allow-motion t)
</code></pre>
</div><br>
<p>
The top-of-buffer ritual was not a huge cost, but it was a repeated one. I do this dozens of times a day, and every time I did it I was leaving the place I wanted to search from, just to make the first match the first match. <code>isearch-allow-motion</code> lets me keep my starting point and still get the first or last match when I need it.
</p>

<p>
It is a small, built-in option, not a new package or a new search interface. It just teaches a few familiar keys to do the obvious thing while isearch is active. If you are on Emacs 28.1 or later, give it a try!
</p>
